1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,200
DAD. IT'S THE SOAP THING AGAIN. I CAN'T EAT THIS.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,400
JADA. WE TALKED ABOUT THIS.

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:08,100
TALKED ABOUT WHAT?

4
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:12,000
YOU'RE PUTTING ME IN A HOME?

5
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:15,300
MAMA. WE WERE GONNA WAIT TILL AFTER CAKE.

6
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:16,900
ON MY BIRTHDAY?

7
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:21,800
SOAP. YOU THINK I CAN'T RINSE A POT?

8
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,700
EVERY DINNER SINCE CHRISTMAS. WE'VE ALL NOTICED.

9
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:26,400
ALL OF YOU?

10
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:29,200
TASTE BUDS GO AS YOU GET OLDER.

11
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:34,100
BUT IF YOU CAN'T TASTE SOAP, WHAT HAPPENS WHEN YOU CAN'T SMELL GAS?

12
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:37,700
I'VE COOKED IN THIS KITCHEN FOR FORTY-TWO YEARS.

13
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:42,100
I KNOW. THAT'S WHY I DON'T WANT YOU IN IT ALONE.

14
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:46,700
WRENFIELD HAS A UNIT OPENING THIS MONTH. I PUT DOWN A DEPOSIT.

15
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:48,500
YOU ALREADY PAID?

16
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:51,500
NO, SHE WON'T FIGHT IT. SHE'S — SLIPPING.

17
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:56,200
GRANDMA, IT'S STILL THERE.

18
00:00:56,200 --> 00:01:00,300
I SCRUBBED THAT PAN THREE TIMES. NEW SOAP. NEW SPONGE.

19
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:02,800
AND I CAN'T TASTE A THING.

20
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:05,200
JADA, AM I LOSING IT?

21
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:08,600
I LOVE YOU, GRANDMA.

22
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,200
WASN'T SHE THE ONE PUSHING YOU INTO WRENFIELD?

23
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:16,500
SHE SURE WAS.

24
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:20,500
NOW SHE'S HERE EVERY SUNDAY, TAKING PLATES HOME. WHAT CHANGED?

25
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,800
SHE TOLD ME MY COOKING TASTED LIKE SOAP.

26
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:26,200
MINE TOO.

27
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:28,000
SHOW ME YOUR KITCHEN.

28
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:29,700
WHEN'D YOU BUY THESE?

29
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:33,700
AFTER THANKSGIVING. TERRANCE SENT ME AN ARTICLE ABOUT BLACK PLASTIC.

30
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:36,200
AND WHEN DID THE SOAP START?

31
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:38,500
CHRISTMAS.

32
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:39,700
SMELL IT.

33
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:41,500
THAT'S MY DISH SOAP.

34
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:44,300
IT SOAKS UP WHATEVER SOAP YOU USE.

35
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:47,500
THE MINUTE IT GETS HOT, OUT IT COMES.

36
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:49,600
BUT I CAN'T TASTE IT.

37
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:53,200
NEITHER COULD I. COOK'S ALWAYS THE LAST TO NOTICE.

38
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:57,400
THEY TESTED SILICONE BAKING PANS. IN THE OVEN, CHEMICALS LEACHED OUT.

39
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:00,700
SOME OF THEM ARE LINKED TO HORMONE DISRUPTION.

40
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:05,700
I THREW ALL MINE OUT AND GOT THESE.

41
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:08,900
WOOD? THE CHEAP ONES BLEED IN THE POT.

42
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:11,300
THAT'S THE FINISH, NOT THE WOOD.

43
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:15,1000
EVERY PIECE IS ONE BLOCK OF WOOD. NO GLUE. NOTHING PAINTED ON.

44
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,600
THEY'RE CALLED ACAWOOD. I JUST TEXTED YOU THE LINK.

45
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,200
KEEP THESE TILL YOURS COME.

46
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:26,600
MOVERS COME SATURDAY AT NINE, MAMA.

47
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:27,900
SMELL THIS FIRST.

48
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,500
MAMA. THIS ISN'T ABOUT A SPATULA.

49
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:31,900
SMELL IT, TERRANCE.

50
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:34,500
THAT'S DISH SOAP.

51
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:37,800
NOW DOROTHY'S. SHE COOKS WITH IT EVERY DAY.

52
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:38,1000
NOTHING.

53
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,500
ONE SNIFF. MY NOSE IS FINE.

54
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:44,700
YOU'RE THE ONE WHO SENT ME THAT ARTICLE.

55
00:02:44,700 --> 00:02:47,300
SMELLING'S ONE THING, MAMA. TASTING'S ANOTHER.

56
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:50,900
FRIDAY NIGHT, I'M MAKING MY POTATO SALAD. EVERYBODY COMES.

57
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:56,600
IF ANYBODY TASTES SOAP, I'LL GET IN YOUR CAR SATURDAY AND NEVER SAY A WORD.

58
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:57,900
DEAL.

59
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:02,800
JADA. YOU FIRST. YOU'RE THE HONEST ONE.

60
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:05,900
GRANDMA.

61
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:07,800
THERE'S NO SOAP. NONE.

62
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:11,400
MAMA. I'M SO SORRY.

63
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:14,800
UNPACK MY BOWLS, TERRANCE.

64
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:15,900
SECONDS?

65
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:22,400
MY SON ALMOST MOVED ME OUT OF MY OWN KITCHEN. OVER THIS.

66
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:26,600
BEFORE ANYBODY TELLS YOU YOU'VE LOST YOUR TOUCH, SMELL YOUR SPATULA.

67
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:30,500
IF IT SMELLS LIKE DISH SOAP, SO DOES YOUR DINNER.

68
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:36,200
AND IF IT'S YOUR WIFE'S COOKING, OR YOUR MAMA'S? SMELL HERS BEFORE YOU BLAME HER.

69
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:41,900
MINE ARE ACAWOOD NOW. SEVEN PIECES, EACH ONE CUT FROM A SINGLE BLOCK OF WOOD.

70
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:47,400
NOTHING ON THEM BUT OIL OR WAX. HERE'S ONE IN HOT WATER. STILL CLEAR.

71
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:49,100
HAND WASH, DRY, DONE.

72
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:55,600
EIGHTY-NINE NINETY-FIVE WITH THE HOLDER. FREE SHIPPING IN THE U.S., AND NINETY-DAY RETURNS.

73
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:59,500
THE LINK'S RIGHT BELOW. AND MY KITCHEN? STILL MINE.

